@flexiarg math-formalization/bijectivity-from-injectivity-plus-count
@title Bijectivity from Injectivity plus a Count
@sigils [🧮/双]
@keywords bijective, injective, surjective, cardinality, card, finite, Fintype, count, equiv
@audience lean-provers
@tone heuristic
@difficulty easy
@subjects algebra combinatorics formalization
@examples b01A04
@provenance case-1 compression pilot (2026-08-12): 4 independent zai-1 marks

! conclusion:
  In a finite setting, never construct a surjection or inverse by hand:
  prove injectivity (usually from a kernel computation) and equality of
  cardinalities (usually from independent counting), and let
  Nat.bijective_iff_injective_and_card conclude bijectivity.

  + context: You need an equivalence between finite structures (e.g.
    MulEquiv.ofBijective) and the surjectivity direction looks like it
    demands explicit preimages.

  + IF: Both sides are Fintype and you can compute or transport both
    cardinalities — often by two INDEPENDENT identities (one for the
    kernel/injectivity, one for the count), which decomposes the claim
    into separately-provable halves.

  + HOWEVER: The cardinality equality must come from somewhere honest —
    if the only route to the count is via the bijection you are
    constructing, the argument is circular. And in infinite settings the
    move is simply false; check finiteness is real, not assumed.

  + THEN: Split: (1) injective_iff_map_eq_one or a kernel argument;
    (2) card equality by counting both sides through known formulas
    (totients, index formulas, orbit counts); (3) apply the
    bijective_iff lemma; (4) bundle with ofBijective.

  + BECAUSE: Explicit inverse construction in dependent-type settings
    costs elaboration friction quadratic in structure depth; counting
    is linear and reuses arithmetic already in the development.
